From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:42:52 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] os, syscall: hold a link to its type where the kernel follows it regardless

A Windows link has a type. A junction and a directory symlink are directory
links, a file symlink is a file link. Windows 10 and later hold a link to
its type when they open it with FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS, and Windows 7, 8
and 8.1 follow it to whatever is there. Without that flag every Windows refuses
a directory entry, so writes through one fail alike. The os.Root comparison
tests (CL 847305, Go 1.28, 1.27.2) hold every plain os call on such a link
to the newer answer.

	open of                          Windows 10, 11   Windows 7, 8, 8.1
	junction or dir symlink -> file  errno 267        the file opens
	file symlink -> directory        errno 5          the directory opens
	file symlink -> file             ok               ok

Open, Utimes and UtimesNano look at what a successful open with the flag
went through on those kernels. A directory link followed to anything but a
directory gets the first answer, and a file symlink followed to a directory
entry gets the second, which means reading the link's target when the
handle is a file. Stat has the link's attributes in hand and answers the
same way. os.Root is not affected, since it resolves links itself.

The difference can only be observed by creating such a link, which nothing
here may do, so the check is gated on the kernel version, like 0004 and
0009. It costs one attribute query per successful open with the flag on
those kernels and nothing elsewhere.

